!AddrSpace - what is using the logical address space     0.1.0
===============================================================

AddrSpace shows what is using RISC OS's logical address space, the
4GB of addresses that all memory has to be fitted into. When it runs
out, programs stop with "Unable to allocate logical address space"
however much memory is free.

This is the first release. Please report problems at
https://github.com/adyoull/riscos-addrspace/issues

Why it runs out
---------------
Every dynamic area (the blocks of memory listed in the Task Manager's
window) takes a range of addresses as big as the most it can ever grow
to, when it is made, however little it holds. So an area that holds
4MB but could grow to 128MB uses 128MB of address space.

RISC OS 5 caps that maximum for each new area (the DALimit setting in
Choices: 128MB unless it has been changed). Programs built with
UnixLib (Matinee, ReelEGL, Reel and many ports) usually keep their
memory in an area called "<program> Heap", which
then reserves 128MB, and "<program> Heap 2", "Heap 3"... as it grows,
another 128MB each. A few programs like that running together, on a
machine with a lot of memory (whose own areas, such as the RAM disc or
the screen, may be large too), can use up the 4GB.

Starting
--------
Double-click !AddrSpace: its icon (a column of memory under a
magnifying glass) appears on the icon bar. Click Select on it to open
its window.

The window
----------
The first three lines are the totals:

  - how many dynamic areas there are, how much address space they
    reserve, and how much memory is in them
  - how much address space is free, once measured (see below), and
    how many more areas of the largest size a new area can have would
    fit
  - the most a new area can reserve now (the cap), how many tasks are
    running, and how often the list is read

Then the list of areas, read again every 2 seconds:

  Name       the area's name
  Owner      the task it probably belongs to: a running task whose
             name its name starts with. "RISC OS" for RISC OS's own
             areas. A name in brackets, in red, is a program that
             isn't running.
  Size       the memory in it now
  Reserved   the address space it takes: the most it can grow to
             (twice that for a doubly-mapped area)
  Base       where its addresses start
  Notes      what changed, and what it is

Click a column's heading to sort the list by it; click again to turn it
round. At first it is sorted by Reserved, the biggest first.

Changes are coloured, for 30 seconds:

  green        a new area
  yellow       an area that grew (Notes says from what)
  blue         an area that shrank
  grey         an area that has gone

A UnixLib program's heap whose program isn't running is shown in red,
"left behind?". When a program quits, its areas should go with it; one
that stays is still taking its address space. (It may also be a program
whose task name isn't the start of its heap's name: check the Task
Manager.) "mmap#" areas are made by the ARMEABISupport module for a
program's memory; they too should go when the program quits.

The menu
--------
Click Menu on the icon or the window.

  Info                 about AddrSpace
  Read every           how often the list is read: 1 to 30 seconds
  Read now             read it now
  Measure free space   measures the free address space, the only sure
                       way: AddrSpace makes empty areas, each as big as
                       RISC OS will allow (halving the size down to 1MB
                       when there's no room), until no more will fit,
                       adds up their sizes, then removes them all. It
                       takes a moment; while it does, there is no
                       address space left for anything else.
  Save list            writes everything (the totals, the list, the
                       totals for each owner and the tasks running) to
                       a text file, <Wimp$ScrapDir>.AddrSpaceList, and
                       opens it
  Log changes          writes the list, then every change as it's seen
                       (with the time), to <Wimp$ScrapDir>.AddrSpaceLog.
                       It keeps logging while the window is closed, and
                       each Measure free space is logged too.
  Quit

To log from the moment AddrSpace starts (for example from the boot
sequence), *Set AddrSpace$Log on before running it.

Finding what uses the address space
-----------------------------------
  1. After starting the computer, run AddrSpace, Measure free space,
     and Save list.
  2. Turn on Log changes, then start the programs one at a time,
     measuring the free space after each.
  3. When "Unable to allocate logical address space" appears, measure
     again and Save list.
  4. Quit the programs one at a time: the areas each one leaves behind
     show in the log as not gone.

The saved list and the log are plain text, to send with a report.

AddrSpace itself
----------------
AddrSpace doesn't change what it measures: its memory is in its own
WimpSlot (it makes no dynamic area), and the only areas it makes are
Measure free space's, which it removes at once. Like every UnixLib
program, its stack is kept by the ARMEABISupport module.

It reads the list with OS_DynamicArea 3 and 24 (2 on a RISC OS without
24), the cap with OS_DynamicArea 8, and the tasks with
TaskManager_EnumerateTasks.

Needs
-----
  - RISC OS 5 on an ARMv7 machine (Raspberry Pi 2, 3, 4, 400 and so on)
  - SharedUnixLibrary 1.16 or later and ARMEABISupport (both from
    PackMan)

